+\section{Conclusion} For a leaderboard whose gold labels are the plurality aggregation of \eqref{eq:loo} and whose gold-generating pool is a strict subset of the ranked field, this audit emits a readable verdict only when the leader clears the producer floor; that floor is determined by choices which are usually unstated. Both conditions are load-bearing, and none of the five readings this paper reports satisfies them together: the two two-generator corpora fail the first, the construction and the released panel fail the second, and the human-gold set has no model-generated gold at all. On the corpora we measured, those choices change what the verdict is worth rather than which verdict it is: both gold definitions leave the question corpus refused rather than decided, the majority reading keeps a margin whose interval excludes zero while the comparison that would have to carry it is refused, and on the decision corpus the point difference clears the declared threshold while its interval contains zero --- which is why the instrument reports a refusal there too. The latent-variable alternative moves the floor in opposite directions on different tasks. The floor and the leaderboard must be measured on the same items for that margin to mean anything, and a margin without an interval is a number rather than a measurement; in our measurements the item sets the labels support are smaller than the corpora and are named, and every reported margin carries the interval that says how little it settles. We release the measurement, three rulers with an explicit refusal when the annotations fail to support the quantity requested, a construction on which the same instrument pins every arm at a margin of zero, and a mutation battery that measures how much of our own test suite can fail. It has now been pointed at one public leaderboard of the shape it was built for: a preregistered disjoint-field deployment (\S\ref{sec:ajb}) whose six judges and five contestants share no members re